        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most garage floor cracks are harmless shrinkage cracks, and most of them can be repaired in an afternoon. The catch is that a coating does not hide a crack, it advertises it. Fix the crack the right way, treat control joints as joints rather than cracks, and grind everything flush, and a new epoxy or polyurea floor will look like one continuous surface. Skip those steps and the crack is back within a season.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="why-it-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Cracks Matter More Under a Coating
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cracks Telegraph Through Thin Coatings</h3>
          <p>
            On bare concrete, a hairline crack is mostly cosmetic. Dirt settles into it, you sweep over it, and nobody notices. Under a glossy coating the story changes. A typical DIY epoxy system goes down somewhere around 8 to 20 mils thick, which is thinner than a credit card in many places. That film follows the surface beneath it exactly, so any open crack shows up as a dark line, a dip, or a sharp edge in the finish, and a gloss coat makes the line more visible, not less.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Adhesion Fails at the Gap</h3>
          <p>
            The second problem is adhesion. A crack is a gap the coating has to bridge with nothing underneath it. Water vapor, dust and old oil wick up through cracks more readily than through sound concrete, and those are the exact things that break the bond between coating and slab. Installers commonly report that delamination on an otherwise good floor often starts along an untreated crack or joint and spreads outward from there. If you have ever seen <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-peeling/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">epoxy peeling off a garage floor</a>, the edges of cracks are a frequent starting point.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Slab Keeps Moving</h3>
          <p>
            The third problem is movement. Concrete grows and shrinks with temperature and moisture, and a garage slab sees larger swings than most indoor floors because the big door lets in winter air and summer heat. Any crack that is still opening and closing will tear a rigid coating along its length. This is called reflective cracking: the crack reflects up through whatever is on top of it. No coating product prevents it on its own, and no amount of extra coats will hide a crack that is still moving.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the job is not just to fill a crack. It is to decide what kind of crack it is, fill the dormant ones with something rigid that bonds and grinds flush, treat the moving ones as joints, and make sure the slab is dry enough for a coating at all. Done in that order, crack repair is a small part of the total project, usually a few hours out of a weekend. Done out of order, it is the reason a floor that cost several hundred dollars in materials needs patching in a year.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Reference Figures</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">8-20 mils</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical DIY epoxy film thickness</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1/16 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Width where a crack starts to show through a coating</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1/4 slab</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common saw-cut control joint depth</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1/8 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Height offset that suggests more than shrinkage</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="read-the-crack" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Read the Crack Before You Pick a Filler
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before you buy anything, walk the floor with a flashlight held low and a pencil. Mark every crack, then sort them into four groups. The sorting decides the material, and getting it wrong is the most common reason a garage floor crack repair fails. The general approach is the same one we use in our <a href="/concrete-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete crack repair guide</a>, applied to the special case of a floor you are about to coat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hairline Shrinkage Cracks</h3>
          <p>
            Hairline shrinkage cracks are the most common. They are thinner than a credit card edge, often wander randomly or radiate from corners and drains, and show no height difference across the crack. They formed as the slab dried in its first months and have usually stopped growing. These are the easy ones. A primer coat or a thin slurry of epoxy and fine silica worked into them is often enough, though anything you can catch a fingernail in deserves to be chased and filled.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wider Dormant Cracks</h3>
          <p>
            Wider dormant cracks, roughly 1/16 inch to 1/4 inch, are the main target of this article. If both sides are level with each other and the crack has not visibly changed in a year or two, treat it as dormant. Chasing it open and filling it with a rigid epoxy or polyurea crack filler is the standard approach. To check whether a crack is still moving, glue a strip of glass slide or draw a pencil line across it and look again after a few weeks of weather swings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Active and Structural Cracks</h3>
          <p>
            Active and structural cracks are different. Warning signs include one side sitting higher than the other by more than about 1/8 inch, cracks that are visibly wider at one end, a crack that keeps reopening after previous repairs, or a pattern of cracks around a sinking section of floor. These suggest settlement, frost heave or poor subgrade. They can still be coated, but a rigid filler will crack again, and some situations call for leveling, <a href="/mudjacking-vs-polyjacking/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">mudjacking or polyjacking</a>, or a professional opinion first.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Crack type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical look</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Repair before coating</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hairline shrinkage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thin, random, level both sides</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Prime or slurry; chase if a fingernail catches</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dormant, 1/16 to 1/4 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Level edges, unchanged over time</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Chase, fill with epoxy or polyurea, grind flush</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Control joint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Straight saw cut or tooled groove</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Keep as a joint; flexible filler or re-cut</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Active or offset</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Height difference, widening, reopens</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flexible treatment or fix the cause first</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="control-joints" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Control Joints Are Not Cracks, So Do Not Fill Them Rigidly
          </h2>

          <p>
            The straight lines cut or tooled into a garage slab are control joints. They are deliberate weak planes, usually cut to about a quarter of the slab depth, that tell the concrete where to crack as it shrinks. Look closely into an old joint and you will often see a hairline crack running along its bottom. That is the joint doing its job. It also means the joint will keep opening and closing slightly with every season for the life of the floor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Rigid Filler in a Joint Fails</h3>
          <p>
            Filling a control joint flush with rigid epoxy, then coating straight across it, locks two pieces of slab together that are designed to move independently. The usual result is a crisp crack running straight down the middle of the filled joint within a winter or two, often with chipped coating along both edges. It looks worse than the open joint did. Several coating manufacturers say plainly in their instructions not to bridge control joints with rigid material, and installers widely treat it as a basic rule.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three Ways to Handle Joints</h3>
          <p>
            There are three sensible ways to handle joints. The simplest is to clean them, coat right into them so the coating wraps the edges, and leave them open as visible grooves. The second is to fill them with a flexible, semi-rigid polyurea or polyurethane joint filler, which keeps out dirt and water while still allowing some movement. The third, popular with professional installers, is to fill the joint, coat over it for a seamless look, and then saw-cut the joint again through the cured coating so it can keep moving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Which one you pick is mostly a matter of looks and maintenance. Open grooves collect grit but never cause trouble. A flexible filler looks cleaner and is what most people want, though it may need touching up every few years. The fill-and-recut method gives the most seamless result but requires a saw and a steady hand. What you should not do is use the same rigid crack filler you put in a dormant crack. Joints and random cracks get different materials, even when they sit side by side.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Rigid filler in dormant cracks. Flexible filler, or nothing, in joints.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you remember one thing from this guide, make it this. Most callbacks on coated garage floors trace back to a control joint that was filled with rigid epoxy and coated over as if it were a crack.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="chase-and-fill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Chase and Fill: How to Fix Garage Floor Cracks Before Epoxy
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Chase the Crack</h3>
          <p>
            Crack chasing means opening a crack into a clean V or U-shaped groove so a filler can bond to sound concrete on both sides. The usual tool is an angle grinder fitted with a diamond crack-chasing blade, which is thicker than a cutting blade and shaped to follow a wandering line. A groove roughly 1/4 inch wide and 1/4 to 1/2 inch deep suits most dormant cracks. Wear a respirator rated for silica dust, eye protection and hearing protection, and use a grinder shroud connected to a vacuum if you have one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Follow the Crack and Vacuum</h3>
          <p>
            Follow the crack rather than cutting a straight line beside it, and stop the groove an inch or so past each visible end. Then vacuum the groove thoroughly, ideally with a shop vacuum and a narrow crevice tool, because loose dust in the bottom of the groove is what fillers fail on. If oil has soaked into the crack, scrub it with a degreaser, rinse, and let the concrete dry fully before going further. Any contamination left in the groove becomes a weak layer under your repair.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Choose the Filler</h3>
          <p>
            For the filler itself, there are two main families. Two-part epoxy crack fillers and epoxy pastes are rigid, bond very well, and usually cure overnight. Polyurea and fast-setting hybrid crack fillers cure in well under an hour in many cases, which lets you fill, grind and coat on the same day. Both are designed to be ground flush and coated over. For deeper grooves, a common trick is to fill partway, broadcast clean dry silica sand into the wet filler to bulk it out, then top off with neat filler.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Overfill Slightly</h3>
          <p>
            Overfill slightly, leaving a small crown above the surface, because most fillers shrink a little as they cure and you will grind the excess off anyway. Avoid feathering filler out over the surrounding slab in a thin smear; that film will be ground through and leave a ragged edge. If you are using a coating kit, check whether the manufacturer offers a matching crack filler; products from the same system are designed to bond to each other, which takes one variable out of the job. Our <a href="/concrete-repair-products/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">guide to concrete repair products</a> sorts the filler types by damage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Chipped Edges and Small Spalls</h3>
          <p>
            Chipped edges along joints and small spalls get the same treatment: grind out loose material back to sound concrete, vacuum, and fill with epoxy mortar or the crack filler thickened with sand. Larger holes and divots may need a dedicated patching compound. If you are buying a <a href="/concrete-repair-kit/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete repair kit</a>, check that it is rated to be coated over, since some cementitious patches need several days of cure before any epoxy can go on top.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moisture-and-cure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Moisture, Temperature and Cure Times
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Moisture From Below</h3>
          <p>
            Garage slabs sit directly on the ground, and many were poured without a vapor barrier underneath. Moisture moving up through the slab is a leading cause of coating failure, and cracks are where it moves most freely. Before any repair or coating, run the plastic sheet test: tape an 18 by 18 inch square of clear plastic tightly to the floor, wait 24 hours, and look for condensation under the plastic or darkened concrete. Our <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-moisture-test/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">garage floor moisture test guide</a> covers the more precise calcium chloride and RH probe methods.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the slab is wet, crack repair alone will not save the coating. You either need a moisture-tolerant primer designed for damp slabs, or you need to fix the water source first, whether that is poor grading, a downspout dumping beside the garage, or a missing vapor barrier. A crack that is visibly damp or wicking water also needs to dry out before most epoxy fillers will bond to it, though some polyurea and polyurethane products are formulated to tolerate damp substrates. Read the label rather than assuming.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Temperature</h3>
          <p>
            Temperature matters as much as moisture. Most epoxy and polyurea crack fillers want the concrete and air somewhere around 50 to 90 degrees F, and the slab should be at least 5 degrees above the dew point so condensation does not form on it while the filler cures. A cold garage floor in late fall can sit well below the air temperature, which slows epoxy cure dramatically and can leave it soft and tacky for days. A cheap infrared thermometer pointed at the floor tells you what you actually need to know.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cure Times</h3>
          <p>
            Cure times vary widely by product, so plan the day around the filler you choose. Fast polyurea fillers often reach grindable hardness in roughly 30 to 60 minutes at room temperature according to their labels. Conventional two-part epoxies usually need overnight, and in a cool garage they may need a full day before they grind cleanly rather than gumming the disc. Cement-based patch products are the slowest to accept a coating, and many coating manufacturers ask for days of cure on fresh cement patches before priming.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="grind-flush" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Grinding Flush and Prepping the Rest of the Floor
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Grind the Repairs Flush</h3>
          <p>
            Once the filler has cured, grind the crowned repair flush with the slab. A cup wheel on an angle grinder handles a few cracks; a rented floor grinder handles a whole garage and makes sense if you are grinding the floor for profile anyway. The goal is a repair that sits exactly level with the concrete around it. Run your palm across it with your eyes closed. If you can feel an edge, the coating will show it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Profile the Whole Floor</h3>
          <p>
            Crack repair usually happens as part of overall surface preparation. Coatings need an open, slightly rough surface profile to bond, and on most garage floors that means grinding or etching. Grinding is generally the more reliable method on older or sealed slabs, and since you are already grinding the crack repairs, it is often simplest to grind the whole floor. Our comparison of <a href="/acid-etching-vs-grinding-garage-floor/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">acid etching vs grinding</a> explains when each method is worth it, and the full sequence is in <a href="/how-to-prepare-concrete-for-epoxy/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to prepare concrete for epoxy</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Getting the Order Right</h3>
          <p>
            Order matters here. Fill cracks after the bulk of the oil and old sealer is removed, but before the final grinding pass, so the repairs and the surrounding concrete get the same profile at the same time. Then vacuum the entire floor, including the bottom of every joint, and wipe it with a dry microfiber mop. Any dust left behind sits between the slab and the primer. Many installers do the final vacuum twice, an hour apart, to catch dust that settles out of the air.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Final Walk-Through</h3>
          <p>
            Before you open the coating, walk the floor once more with the low flashlight. Repaired cracks should be invisible to your fingertips and the joints should be either clean and open or filled with flexible material, as you planned. If you are using a system from our <a href="/best-epoxy-garage-floor-kit/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best epoxy garage floor kit</a> picks, check whether the kit calls for a primer coat first; a primer seals the slab and repair together and usually improves how a repaired crack looks under the color coat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reflective-cracking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Reflective Cracking and When Cracks Come Back
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Repairs Reappear</h3>
          <p>
            Even a careful repair can reappear. If a crack you judged dormant was actually still moving, the rigid filler will hold the edges together until the slab pulls harder than the concrete beside the repair can resist, and then a new hairline crack forms, usually right along the edge of the filler or straight through it. This is not a defect in the filler. It is the slab telling you the crack is active, and it is the most common reason a coated garage floor shows a line again in its first winter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Living With Cracks That Still Move</h3>
          <p>
            There are honest ways to live with this. For cracks you suspect are still moving, use a flexible joint filler instead of a rigid one and accept that the repair will look like a joint. Alternatively, treat the line as a new control joint: after coating, saw-cut a straight joint along the crack path so future movement happens there. Some flooring contractors also install crack isolation details or reinforcing fabric over moving cracks, though that is beyond most DIY jobs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When the Problem Is Under the Slab</h3>
          <p>
            If cracks keep returning and growing, the problem is under the slab, not in it. Washed-out fill, a sinking section near the door, frost heave at the apron, or water collecting under the floor can all keep a crack active. Pay attention to whether one side is dropping, whether the garage door no longer seals at the bottom, and whether the cracks connect into a pattern. At that point a coating is the last step, not the next one, and a <a href="/concrete-leveling-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete leveling estimate</a> may be money better spent.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the overwhelming majority of garage floors, though, the cracks are shrinkage cracks that stopped growing years ago. Chased, filled with a rigid epoxy or polyurea filler, ground flush and coated, they disappear. Pair that with flexible joints and a dry slab and the floor will look the same in five years as it does the week you finish. If your cracks are outdoors or in flat traffic areas where flexibility matters more than invisibility, a <a href="/self-leveling-concrete-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">self-leveling concrete crack filler</a> is often the better product, and the top choices are in our <a href="/best-concrete-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete crack filler rankings</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I have to fix garage floor cracks before epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For anything wider than a hairline, yes. Epoxy films are thin and follow the surface exactly, so open cracks show through as lines or dips and are common starting points for peeling. Hairline cracks can often be handled by the primer coat, but cracks you can catch a fingernail in should be chased, filled and ground flush first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best filler for garage floor cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For dormant cracks under a coating, a rigid two-part epoxy or a fast polyurea crack filler that is rated to be ground and coated over. For control joints and cracks that are still moving, a flexible polyurea or polyurethane joint filler is better. Our pillar guide ranks specific crack fillers by the kind of crack they suit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I fill the control joints in my garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can fill them, but not with rigid epoxy. Use a semi-rigid or flexible joint filler that allows movement, or coat into the joint and leave it open. Some installers fill, coat over for a seamless look and then saw-cut the joint again. Bridging a joint with rigid material usually causes a new crack in the coating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should crack filler cure before coating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends entirely on the product and the slab temperature. Fast polyurea fillers often grind cleanly within about an hour at room temperature, while two-part epoxies usually need overnight or longer in a cold garage. Cement-based patches can need days. Follow the label, and measure the floor temperature rather than the air.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will cracks come back through new epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Dormant cracks that are chased, filled and ground flush usually stay hidden. Cracks that are still moving will reflect through any rigid coating, typically appearing as a new hairline along the repair. If a crack reopens after one repair, treat it as an active crack and switch to a flexible joint treatment.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use regular concrete caulk on a garage floor I plan to coat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Many flexible caulks and self-leveling sealants are not designed to be coated over, and some prevent epoxy from bonding at all. They are excellent for exposed joints and outdoor slabs. Under a coating, use a crack filler the coating manufacturer approves, or test a small area before committing the whole floor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is a garage floor crack a structural problem?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Worry when one side sits more than about 1/8 inch higher than the other, when cracks widen over months, when a section of floor is visibly sinking, or when the door no longer seals at the bottom. Those point to settlement, heave or washed-out fill beneath the slab, which need attention before any cosmetic repair or coating.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling control joints with rigid epoxy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A control joint is designed to move. Filling it flush with rigid epoxy and coating over it almost guarantees a sharp crack down the middle within a season or two, usually with chipped coating along both edges. Use a flexible joint filler, leave the joint open, or re-cut it after coating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the crack chase</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Squeezing filler into an unopened hairline leaves most of the material sitting on top, bonded to dust and laitance rather than sound concrete. Chasing the crack to about 1/4 inch wide gives the filler two clean faces to grip. It takes minutes per crack and decides whether the repair lasts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating before the filler has cured</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epoxy fillers in a cool garage can stay soft for a day or more. Grinding them early gums the disc and leaves a low spot, and coating over uncured filler can cause soft areas and discoloration. Check the label cure time at your actual slab temperature, not the room temperature.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring moisture coming up through cracks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A perfect crack repair on a damp slab still fails, because vapor pushes the coating off from below. Run a plastic sheet test before you start. If moisture shows up, fix drainage or choose a moisture-tolerant primer before spending time on cosmetic crack work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the repair proud of the floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A crown of filler you can feel with your hand will show through the coating as a raised ridge, especially under a gloss finish with overhead lighting. Grind every repair flush and check by touch with your eyes closed. If you can feel it, you will see it once the color coat goes on.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

